                    Visibility="Collapsed"/>

            <!-- The splitter row's VISIBLE part is exactly this 1px divider -
                 which is why the tree's scrollbar (and first row) start flush
                 beneath the line. The line used to be FavoritesList's own
                 bottom border with the transparent grab zone taking 4px of
                 real space after it, leaving a visible gap between the line
                 and everything below. Position-mode flipping of that border
                 is gone too: the line IS the boundary now, same on either
                 side. -->
            <!-- Back to 1px, matching every other section divider (
                 2026-08-08). It spent 08-04~08-08 at 2px ("a hairline between
                 two alike lists read as a gap"), but once the viewer panel
                 added another 1px line the odd one out was this one. The
                 splitter's grab zone is unaffected - it floats over this row
                 at a fixed 9px and simply centres on whatever height the
                 line takes. -->
            <Border Grid.Row="2"
                    Grid.Column="1"
                    Height="1"
                    Background="{DynamicResource PanelDividerBrush}"/>
            <!-- Functionality unchanged (drag-resize, double-click fit); only
                 WHERE the grab zone lives did: negative margins float it over
                 the last few pixels of the panels on either side of the line
                 instead of occupying its own space (net desired height: 1px,
                 so the Auto row stays exactly the line). ZIndex wins the
                 overlapped strip's hit-testing from the rows beneath. -->
            <GridSplitter x:Name="FavoritesSplitter"
                    Grid.Row="2"
                    Grid.Column="1"
                    Height="9"
                    Margin="0,-4,0,-4"
                    Panel.ZIndex="1"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center"
                    Background="Transparent"
                    ResizeDirection="Rows"
                    DragCompleted="FavoritesSplitter_DragCompleted"
                    MouseDoubleClick="FavoritesSplitter_MouseDoubleClick"/>

            <!-- ScrollUnit="Pixel": without it, ScrollUnit defaults to Item,
                 which with CanContentScroll=True (see ExplorerTreeViewStyle's
                 ScrollViewer) makes this root panel scroll in units of whole
                 drive-root items. An expanded C: rendering 2000px tall still
                 counts as exactly one unit there, so the scrollbar moved in a
                 few coarse steps and scroll offsets were meaningless as
                 pixels. Pixel makes the extent the actual rendered height.

                 CacheLength="1000,1000" with CacheLengthUnit="Item" is what
                 fixes favorites navigation to a DIFFERENT drive. Temporary
                 diagnostic logging finally pinned it down: the walk was
                 failing at its very first step, on the drive root itself -
                 e.g. "step[0] 'E:' containerFound=False" repeated until it
                 gave up - while every deeper step succeeded on its first try.
                 With C: expanded to ~2165px and the viewport only ~432px tall
                 scrolled to ~595px, the E: row sits ~2165px down, far outside
                 the default cache (one viewport-page either side), so its
                 container was never realized and ContainerFromItem returned
                 null forever. That is the real reason this only reproduced in
                 floating mode (short viewport -> small cache) and got better
                 as the per-folder display cap shrank (shorter C: -> E: closer)
                 - a hard structural miss, not the timing flake several earlier
                 rounds of retry-tuning assumed. There are only ever a handful
                 of drive roots, so caching "all of them" costs nothing.

                 Both are deliberately blocked from inheriting down to
                 individual folders - see ExplorerTreeViewItemStyle, which
                 re-sets CacheLength locally. Folders keep normal caching:
                 they were never the problem (the log proves it), and letting
                 a "reveal everything" cache reach a folder someone expanded
                 via "더 보기" would realize hundreds of rows for no reason. -->
            <!-- VirtualizationMode is Standard, not Recycling. Recycling reuses
                 row containers as they scroll out of view, and a reused
                 container whose visual fails to refresh renders as a blank gap
                 - one candidate for the reported "rows disappear from the
                 middle of the tree until I click something". The saving it
                 offers is small here regardless, since CacheLength already
                 keeps a thousand containers alive. This is a SEPARATE setting
                 from CacheLength below; that one fixes favorites navigation and
                 must stay as it is.

                 Also note: do NOT "fix" this by setting IsVirtualizing=False
                 here. That was tried; it silently un-virtualizes the ENTIRE
                 tree (the property inherits) and breaks scrolling app-wide. -->
            <!-- EXPERIMENT 2026-08-10, NOT a decision: ScrollUnit below was
                 "Pixel". In pixel mode the panel has to guess the height of
                 every row it hasn't realized, from the average of the ones it
                 has - and in a tree that average is meaningless, since a file
                 row is ~28px and an expanded folder's container is thousands.
                 Measured: the same folders reported 9,081px tall one moment and
                 36,072px the next, from ~290 realized rows out of ~1,200. Rows
                 arranged from those guesses land on top of each other, which is
                 what a filter toggle was showing for about a second.
                 Item mode has nothing to guess.
                 If this proves the cause, PinRowToTop and the bottom-gap
                 arithmetic must be reworked before it can stay - both are built
                 on exact pixel offsets (see PinRowToTop's own comment). -->
            <TreeView x:Name="ExplorerTree"
                    Grid.Row="3"
                    Grid.Column="1"
                    Style="{StaticResource ExplorerTreeViewStyle}"
                      Background="{DynamicResource SidebarBackground}"
                      Foreground="{DynamicResource FolderNameForeground}"
                    FontSize="12"
                    BorderThickness="0"
                    ContextMenu="{StaticResource ExplorerEmptySpaceContextMenu}"
                      ItemContainerStyle="{StaticResource ExplorerTreeViewItemStyle}"
                      VirtualizingPanel.IsVirtualizing="True"
                      VirtualizingPanel.VirtualizationMode="Standard"
                      VirtualizingPanel.ScrollUnit="Item"
                      VirtualizingPanel.CacheLengthUnit="Item"
                      VirtualizingPanel.CacheLength="1000,1000"
                      ScrollViewer.HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                      MouseDoubleClick="ExplorerTree_MouseDoubleClick"
                      PreviewMouseLeftButtonDown="ExplorerTree_PreviewMouseLeftButtonDown"
                      TreeViewItem.Expanded="ExplorerTree_ItemExpandedOrCollapsed"
                      TreeViewItem.Collapsed="ExplorerTree_ItemExpandedOrCollapsed"
                      SelectedItemChanged="ExplorerTree_SelectedItemChanged"
                      DragLeave="ExplorerTree_DragLeave"
                      KeyDown="ExplorerTree_KeyDown"
                      PreviewKeyDown="ExplorerTree_PreviewKeyDown"
                      PreviewMouseWheel="ExplorerTree_PreviewMouseWheel"/>

            <!-- Same divider style FavoritesList already draws between itself
                 and the tree below, using the same dedicated "영역 구분선"
                 color (PanelDividerBrush) as that one - anyone who wants it
                 gone can already do that by picking a color that matches the
                 background. Declared last (not right after HeaderGrid) so it
                 paints on top of FavoritesList/ExplorerTree regardless of
                 which one ApplyFavoritesPosition currently has in Row1 -
                 needed because the -2 bottom margin below deliberately
                 overlaps 2px into whichever of them sits in Row1's top edge
                 (see the TODO note: divider 2px lower, header content 1px
                 higher, without moving the header row's own height or the
                 OTHER divider between favorites and the tree). -->
            <!-- Spans all three columns like the header above it, so the line
                 keeps running across the viewer panel's top too. (The column
                 assignment matters at all because the unnamed dividers were
                 the two casualties of the viewer's column split - they sat at
                 the default column 0, which is the zero-width viewer column,
                 and vanished; 2026-08-08.) -->
            <!-- ...and spanning the columns was not enough on its own
                 (2026-08-11). The -2 below puts the line inside ROW 1, where the
                 viewer panel lives, and ViewerPanel is declared further down
                 this file - so over the viewer's column the panel's own
                 background painted straight over it and the line stopped dead at
                 the split. Declaration order cannot fix it here the way it did
                 for the tree: the panel has to come after the thumb and the rest
                 of the split. ZIndex 3 clears both the panel (0) and the split
                 thumb (2). Full screen collapses this Border outright, so the
                 raised line can never appear over a picture. -->
            <Border x:Name="HeaderUnderline"
                    Grid.Row="0"
                    Grid.ColumnSpan="3"
                    Height="1"
                    Panel.ZIndex="3"
                    VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                    Margin="0,0,0,-2"
                    Background="{DynamicResource PanelDividerBrush}"/>

            <!-- Version footer - same dim treatment as the tree's own "더 보기"
                 row (inherited foreground at reduced opacity) rather than its
                 own color, so it reads as a quiet, out-of-the-way label.
                 Hidden along with the rest of the expanded content (see
                 SetExpandedContentVisibility) when collapsed to the icon-only
                 rail - too narrow for this text, which used to just clip. -->
            <Border x:Name="VersionFooterBorder"
                    Grid.Row="4"
                    Grid.Column="1"
                    BorderThickness="0,1,0,0"
                    BorderBrush="{DynamicResource PanelDividerBrush}">
                <!-- The path strip rides INSIDE the footer border rather than
                     in a Grid row of its own. A new row would have meant
                     renumbering the footer and bumping RowSpan on all five
                     elements that span rows 1-4 (search overlay, viewer panel,
                     its split thumb and both chevrons) - five chances to leave
                     one behind, for a strip that is anchored to the bottom
                     edge either way. Stacking it here also gets the rest for
                     free: SetExpandedContentVisibility already hides this
                     border and zeroes the row, and the search overlay already
                     covers it. -->
                <StackPanel x:Name="FooterStack" Orientation="Vertical">
                    <!-- Collapsed unless the option is on (ApplyPathBarVisibility) -
                         a StackPanel child at Collapsed takes no height, so the
                         footer keeps being exactly the chip strip when it's off. -->
                    <!-- Bottom 5 → 2, and the chip strip's bottom 3 → 6. The
                         three pixels are MOVED rather than added: the footer's
                         total height is unchanged, so this row does not shift
                         on screen at all (asked for: lift the chips, leave the
                         path where it is) while the strip below comes off the
                         window's edge. The air was in the wrong half - there
                         was more of it between two things that are obviously
                         separate than under the last row, where the window
                         ends. -->
                    <!-- 트리 히스토리's two buttons live at the right end, OUTSIDE
                         the field's outline: they are not part of what the box
                         accepts. The field's three layers carry no Grid.Column
                         and so stay in the star column they always had. -->
                    <Grid x:Name="PathBarRow"
                            Margin="6,5,6,2"
                            Height="24"
                            Visibility="Collapsed">
                        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
